CD(1)                     Funny CLI Manual                     CD(1)

NAME
    cd — Change the current directory, because staying in one place is boring.

SYNOPSIS
    cd [PATH]

DESCRIPTION
    The cd command changes the current working directory to the specified
    path.

    If no path is provided, cd returns to the workspace directory.

USAGE
    > cd <path>     # Changes to the specified directory.
    > cd            # Returns to the workspace directory.

FUN FACTS
    - cd stands for "change directory", not "cool destination".
    - You can use relative or absolute paths.
    - Using cd .. lets you travel one directory upward.
    - cd without arguments is basically the CLI equivalent of going home.

EXAMPLES
    > cd documents
        Changes the current directory to the "documents" directory.

    > cd /home/user
        Changes the current directory to "/home/user".

    > cd ..
        Moves one directory upward.

    > cd
        Returns to the workspace directory.

SEE ALSO
    help(1), ls(1), pwd(1), man(1)

    Remember, cd is basically teleportation for your filesystem.
    It just takes a little typing.

